---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
---
<Layout title="About" description="How Sean Despain works across product, design, brand, and code.">
  <Header slot="header" />

  <section class="about">
    <div class="container about__inner">
      <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>About</p>
      <h1 data-reveal data-reveal-delay="60">I don't like handing off the parts I understand best.</h1>

      <p data-reveal data-reveal-delay="120">
        Most of what I've built started the same way: a real need, no team assigned to it yet, and
        a decision to just build the thing rather than write a brief for someone else to build it.
        That's how everything on this site started, along with a handful of builds that aren't
        featured here. If you have something that needs to exist, that's usually where I start too.
      </p>

      <p data-reveal data-reveal-delay="160">
        I move between product design, front-end and back-end engineering, brand identity, and
        marketing because in a small venture those aren't separate jobs, they're separate hours of
        the same day. Designing the onboarding flow for a wellness app, writing the Firebase auth
        layer under it, and then building the marketing site that sells it are the same project to
        me, not three handoffs. I've also run the businesses underneath the products: founded and
        staffed a construction company, founded a CBD company, and now direct an international
        nonprofit program on the ground in Peru.
      </p>

      <p data-reveal data-reveal-delay="200">
        That range means I approach a new problem by asking what it actually needs, not what
        discipline I'd prefer to reach for. Sometimes the answer is an interaction-design problem.
        Sometimes it's an outright bug in a Unity build-stripping edge case. Sometimes it's realizing
        a page is about to publish a claim nobody's verified, and the right move is a placeholder,
        not a guess dressed up as a fact. That discipline runs through the Qinty and Once Upon a
        Princess builds especially.
      </p>

      <p data-reveal data-reveal-delay="240">
        I use AI tools heavily in how I build, for prototyping, for code, for content workflows,
        the same way I'd use any other tool that gets a real product shipped faster without lowering
        the bar on what ships. The work on this site is the evidence; the tools behind it are just
        tools.
      </p>

      <div class="about__cta" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="280">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/#work">See the work</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/resume/Sean_Despain_Resume_Hybrid_ProductCreativeTech.docx">Download résumé</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer slot="footer" />

  <script>
    import "../../scripts/reveal.js";
  </script>
</Layout>

<style>
  .about { padding-block: var(--space-6) var(--space-7); }
  .about__inner { max-width: 42rem; }
  .about h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: var(--step-4);
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin: 0.4rem 0 var(--space-4);
  }
  .about p { color: var(--ink); font-size: var(--step-0); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .about__cta { display: flex; gap: var(--space-2); flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: var(--space-4); }
  .btn {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 0.85rem 1.4rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    border: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .btn--primary { background: var(--ink); color: var(--paper); }
  .btn--ghost { background: transparent; color: var(--ink); }
  .btn--ghost:hover { background: var(--line); }
</style>
